<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?> <?xml-stylesheet type="text/xsl" href="rss.xsl"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"> <channel> <title>vanderoost</title><description>I automate tech companies</description><link>https://vanderoost.com/</link><atom:link href="https://vanderoost.com/feed_rss_updated.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml" /> <managingEditor>Richard van der Oost</managingEditor><language>en</language> <pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 17:18:10 -0000</pubDate> <lastBuildDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 17:18:10 -0000</lastBuildDate> <ttl>1440</ttl> <generator>MkDocs RSS plugin - v1.19.0</generator> <image> <url>None</url> <title>vanderoost</title> <link>https://vanderoost.com/</link> </image> <item> <title>The 7 levels of highly effective Makefiles</title> <author>Richard van der Oost</author> <description>Learn how to write a full-on Makefile from scratch to automate building C projects.</description> <link>https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/08/05/levels-of-effective-makefile-cheatsheet/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:40:37 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://vanderoost.com/feed_rss_updated.xml">vanderoost</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/08/05/levels-of-effective-makefile-cheatsheet/</guid> <enclosure url="https://vanderoost.com/assets/images/social/articles/2026/08/05/levels-of-effective-makefile-cheatsheet.png" type="image/png" length="51175" /> </item> <item> <title>Plotting histograms in the terminal</title> <author>Richard van der Oost</author> <description>A simple C library for plotting histograms in the terminal.</description> <link>https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/09/11/histogram-plotting-in-the-terminal/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:40:37 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://vanderoost.com/feed_rss_updated.xml">vanderoost</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/09/11/histogram-plotting-in-the-terminal/</guid> <enclosure url="https://vanderoost.com/assets/images/social/articles/2026/09/11/histogram-plotting-in-the-terminal.png" type="image/png" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Stop using rand()</title> <author>Richard van der Oost</author> <description>&lt;h1&gt;Stop using &lt;code&gt;rand()&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/h1&gt; &lt;div class=&#34;rvo-video&#34;&gt;&lt;iframe src=&#34;https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/-5NqTA-fDNQ&#34; title=&#34;Coding RNG from scratch in C&#34; loading=&#34;lazy&#34; referrerpolicy=&#34;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&#34; allow=&#34;accelerometer; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share&#34; allowfullscreen&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;p&gt;In this article I will try to convince you to stop using &lt;code&gt;rand()&lt;/code&gt; in your C projects, and write a better alternative from scratch.&lt;/p&gt;</description> <link>https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/09/25/stop-using-rand/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:40:37 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://vanderoost.com/feed_rss_updated.xml">vanderoost</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/09/25/stop-using-rand/</guid> <enclosure url="https://vanderoost.com/assets/images/social/articles/2026/09/25/stop-using-rand.png" type="image/png" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Hacking floats to learn how they work</title> <author>Richard van der Oost</author> <description>&lt;h1&gt;Hacking floats to learn how they work&lt;/h1&gt; &lt;div class=&#34;rvo-video&#34;&gt;&lt;iframe src=&#34;https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/ksumuyRAk-U&#34; title=&#34;Learn how floats work in C without reading the spec&#34; loading=&#34;lazy&#34; referrerpolicy=&#34;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&#34; allow=&#34;accelerometer; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share&#34; allowfullscreen&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;p&gt;In this article I want to explore floating-point numbers (floats). Floats are a bit more complicated than integers. It&#39;s quite easy to convert a bunch of bits to an (unsigned) integer, but for floats this is not trivial.&lt;/p&gt;</description> <link>https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/10/02/how-floats-work/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:40:37 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://vanderoost.com/feed_rss_updated.xml">vanderoost</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/10/02/how-floats-work/</guid> <enclosure url="https://vanderoost.com/assets/images/social/articles/2026/10/02/how-floats-work.png" type="image/png" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Generating random floats</title> <author>Richard van der Oost</author> <description>&lt;h1&gt;Generating random floats&lt;/h1&gt; &lt;div class=&#34;rvo-video&#34;&gt;&lt;iframe src=&#34;https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/qa83_KrkVIM&#34; title=&#34;Generating better random floats in C&#34; loading=&#34;lazy&#34; referrerpolicy=&#34;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&#34; allow=&#34;accelerometer; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share&#34; allowfullscreen&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;p&gt;We&#39;ve already hand-rolled our own RNG in a &lt;a href=&#34;../2026-09-14_stop-using-rand/index.md&#34;&gt;previous article&lt;/a&gt;, to improve on what the C standard library &lt;code&gt;rand()&lt;/code&gt; gives us.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;We also talked about &lt;a href=&#34;../2026-10-02_how-floats-work/index.md&#34;&gt;how floats work&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In this article, I want to combine the two. I want to find out how we can generate random floating-point numbers between 0 and 1, and improve our rng.c library.&lt;/p&gt;</description> <link>https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/10/09/better-random-floats/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:40:37 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://vanderoost.com/feed_rss_updated.xml">vanderoost</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/10/09/better-random-floats/</guid> <enclosure url="https://vanderoost.com/assets/images/social/articles/2026/10/09/better-random-floats.png" type="image/png" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Why 95% of company AI projects fail</title> <author>Richard van der Oost</author> <description>&lt;h1&gt;Why 95% of company AI projects fail&lt;/h1&gt; &lt;p&gt;It&#39;s an interesting time to be alive. The &#34;AI transformation&#34; is happening.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Many business owners feel FOMO on AI adoption. Yet, allegedly 95% of AI projects in companies fail, according to an MIT report. Why is this?&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;I&#39;ve talked to business owners and AI automation agencies. This is what I found.&lt;/p&gt;</description> <link>https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/09/13/why-company-ai-projects-fail/</link> <pubDate>Sun, 13 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://vanderoost.com/feed_rss_updated.xml">vanderoost</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://vanderoost.com/articles/2026/09/13/why-company-ai-projects-fail/</guid> <enclosure url="https://vanderoost.com/assets/images/social/articles/2026/09/13/why-company-ai-projects-fail.png" type="image/png" length="None" /> </item> </channel> </rss>